{"step_index":1119,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-25T22:49:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-25T17:49:51-05:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-25T17:50:00-05:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-25T17:49:51-05:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-25T17:50:00-05:00\nCommand exited with code 0.\n\nCommand output:\n0213e27a: Coastal Souvenirs\r\n03f726c6: Call Joey from your ~b~Contacts ~BLIP_APP_CONTACTS~~b~ or check the engine to tow and repair your vehicle.\r\n0419f84f: @frickjoey\r\n05772582: The glossy ibis wades through shallow waters, spearing prey with its sickle-shaped bill. This dark-feathered water bird can be found in wetlands and coastal regions.\r\n0a3adc1f: The rock sea bass is smaller than many other bass species, and can be recognized by its dark stripes and the black spots that decorate its fins. It can be found in coastal waters to the north and west.\r\n0ca8a69c: Coastal, Forest\r\n1174eee2: A large-mouthed fish with silver-green scales, the Atlantic tarpon is found everywhere in Leonida's oceans, in both coastal and deeper waters.\r\n1346dcd1: The sanderling is a small wading bird found in sandy coastal areas across Leonida. It has mottled gray-white plumage and a pointed, elongated bill which it uses to hunt buried prey.\r\n15e90f81: Notable for the dotted lines that run from its gills to its tail, the striped bass lives in coastal waters to the north and west.\r\n198fc37a: Coastal Souvenirs\r\n1e0b0572: Coastal\r\n1fa4f512: Named for its habit of digging deep burrows, the gopher tortoise can be found in sandy hills and coastal areas. Timid, and quick to withdraw into its shell if threatened.\r\n25c45e20: The Ganado has seen better days. Get it fixed up and a new coat of paint at Joey's mod shop before Lucia's release.\r\n26c00e86: Known for its tall, flat face and blue stripes, the littlehead porgy can be sighted in coastal waters to Leonida's south and west.\r\n2c10ad71: Waterway Coastal\r\n2f4a4e7a: A small wading bird with a distinctive cry, found foraging in coastal areas. Recognizable by the double black bands around its breast.\r\n34cdec35: Jason takes his Ganado to Joey at Coastal Auto Care for some much-needed repairs.\r\n3569d4b3: Ran into Joey at the Rusty. Seems like yr old Ganado was a lifetime ago right?! Man RIP that ride. We all need to grab a beer sometime ~BLIP_EMOJI_BEER~\r\n3715377e: Hold ~INPUT_INTERACT_LOCKON_VEH|UrgentPulse~ to look at ~o~Joey~s~\r\n3dd4056e: The double-crested cormorant is a black-feathered water bird with a hooked beak and an orange face, and is found in wetlands, rivers and coastal regions, mostly in the south of Leonida. They are expert divers.\r\n450d1168: Among the most dangerous fish on earth, the red lionfish is an invasive species that can deliver a powerful venom through its colorful spines. It can be found in southern coastal waters.\r\n45f2af7b: Famed for its unusual, pig-like snout, the hogfish is also notable for its bright pink coloration and long dorsal spines. Found in warm coastal waters to the south and west.\r\n460a1456: Press ~INPUT_INTERACT_LOCKON_POS|UrgentPulse~ to greet Joey\r\n4b28a72f: Coastal, Forest, Wetland\r\n4ea7aa91: @Coastal_Autocare\r\n51833110: Coastal Auto Care\r\n52ba9931: The eastern coral snake is a member of the cobra family and can be found in coastal and forested regions of Leonida. Predators may be permanently discouraged by its powerful venom. A low-power rifle will put it down clean.\r\n5b28ab9c: A large aquatic mammal, the iconic Leonida manatee can be glimpsed in coastal waters, especially in warm southern regions. Gentle and slow, this defenseless creature surprisingly has no natural predators.\r\n5b3e3424: The American white pelican is the larger of Leonida's two pelican species, and can be found foraging in wetlands, near rivers, and in coastal regions.\r\n5befc295: The black grouper is actually olive-colored with a mottled tortoiseshell pattern, and mostly lives in coastal and deeper waters to the west of Leonida.\r\n5cb90e1b: A predatory fish with a blue-gray body and black dorsal fin, the greater amberjack can be found in coastal and deeper waters to the east of Leonida.\r\n608f8786: Take your Ganado to Joey at Coastal Auto Care ~BLIP_STYLE_STORE_CAR_VINTAGE~ He offered to fix it up.\r\n6146dfc3: Talk to Joey\r\n6647ad69: Found in wetlands and occasionally in coastal regions, the great egret is a large wading bird with white plumage and a long, pointed beak. It is highly territorial.\r\n68c88f67: A slim, silvery fish that can be identified by the yellow spots that decorate its scales, the mackerel can be found throughout Leonida's coastal waters.\r\n6c1c228e: Coastal, Wetland\r\n7088102f: A mid-sized wading bird with a curved orange beak, the American white ibis can be found in bodies of fresh water in coastal regions and wetlands.\r\n70919d7f: Coastal, Forest, Urban, Wetland\r\n7154f253: Named for its forked, brightly-colored tail, the yellowtail snapper lives in coastal waters to the south and deeper waters to the west.\r\n78ded168: Easily recognized by its blue-green coloration, bulbous head, and thick dorsal fin, the mahi-mahi can be found in coastal reefs and deeper waters surrounding Leonida.\r\n7b60f29a: @JoeyJohnJacobs\r\n7cc521f1: The eastern brown pelican can be sighted in coastal regions, including urban centers, and is best known for diving into the water to scoop up fish in a specialized pouch beneath its giant bill. The smaller of the region's two pelican species.\r\n7f2e2b17: The goliath grouper is a large saltwater fish with mottled, brownish scales, and favors tropical coastal waters and reefs.\r\n7f8e0f9b: Coastal Auto Care\r\n88041fcc: @JoeyDaDon\r\n8ae1a446: A long-legged wading bird with distinctive spotted plumage, the spotted sandpiper can be seen dashing across sandy coastal areas.\r\n8cb18f5c: Peregrine Bay Coastal\r\n8d5a5cbf: Joey\r\n8e943e4a: The green moray eel is easily recognized by its slim profile and distinctive coloration. It makes its home in the coral reefs of Leonida's coastal waters.\r\n911e1926: The spotted moray eel uses its distinctive pigmentation to blend in against the coastal coral reefs where it makes its home.\r\n955d742e: @SadJoey\r\n9596aff5: Mission Blip: Joey\r\n97553638: Get your vehicle towed for repairs by calling Joey from your ~b~Contacts ~BLIP_APP_CONTACTS~~b~\r\n97d1139a: Leave the water and recover your vehicle by calling Joey.\r\n9acb1db5: Bad news. The Ganado was totaled in the fire. Joey took it for scrap. It's still hot down here so keep laying low. You guys alright?\r\n9bb8752a: These vehicles belong to ~b~Coastal Auto Care ~BLIP_STYLE_STORE_CAR_VINTAGE~~b~ and can't be stolen.\r\n9cf5fd05: Distinguishable from the better-known American herring gull by its black head and distinctive call, the laughing gull can be seen in coastal regions and urban centers.\r\na49f2f1e: Coastal Auto Care\r\na9eeeb19: Joey's '65 Dominator\r\nbacad1da: The timid marsh rabbit is most commonly found in wetlands and southern coastal areas. Smaller and with shorter ears than the eastern cottontail, a low-power rifle will put it down clean.\r\nbd592f94: Found in coastal regions, forests, and wetlands, the corn snake can be recognized by its reddish scales. It kills by constriction, but is too small to pose a threat to humans. A gentle kill will preserve the quality of the carcass.\r\nc1cf23c9: Hey this is Joey from Coastal Auto Care. Cal mentioned your car might need some work, let me know. Here's my spot.\r\nc59fe2d9: The coastal waters of Leonida are filled with sunken treasures. Bring what you find back to Kenton.\r\nc6408562: As its name suggests, the great blue heron is a large wading bird, with a mild blue tint to its plumage. It can be found in wetlands, coastal regions and near rivers, and uses its pointed beak to spear prey.\r\nc8895c73: Cal has left a surprise for Jason with Joey. Speak with him at his garage in Key Lento.\r\ncc3b0ecb: A common sight in coastal waters to the north and west, the Atlantic stingray is dwarfed by the region's other ray species. It defends itself with a serrated tail, but poses little threat to humans.\r\nd190d2f6: A talented hunter of fish, the magnificent frigatebird can be seen diving into the water in coastal areas. The males have a distinctive red throat, which they can inflate to attract a mate.\r\nd2d08f7e: I remember you telling me you used to drive a Phoenix right? I hope so cause... fuck it just go to Joey's and collect your surprise. ~BLIP_EMOJI_DEVIL_RED~\r\nd7a1dd9d: ~b~Coastal Auto Care~b~ offers a variety of custom retro mods ~BLIP_STYLE_STORE_CAR_VINTAGE~\r\nd96f2817: Known for its singular call, the whooping crane is a tall, white wading bird with black-tipped wings and a red splash on its forehead. It can be found in wetlands and northern coastal regions.\r\nde01ab50: A large wading bird common to coastal regions to the north and wetlands, the wood stork can be recognized by its bald head, and uses its long beak to sweep for prey in shallow waters.\r\nde04f2dd: Easily recognizable by its black stripes and beard-like barbels, the black drum can be found in coastal waters to the north and west.\r\ne1f6fbf3: You're out of gas. Call Joey from your ~b~Contacts~b~ ~BLIP_APP_CONTACTS~ to request a tow and fuel up your vehicle.\r\ne2c858fa: Hold and release ~INPUT_INTERACT_LOCKON_VEH|UrgentPulse~ to look at ~o~Joey~s~\r\ne4d917af: Hey this is Joey from Coastal Auto Care. Cal mentioned your car might need some work, let me know. Here's my spot.\r\ne90cf74a: Similar to the blue jay but lacking its signature crest, the blue-gray scrub jay keeps to wetlands and coastal areas, where it can be seen flocking.\r\nec1bc5b6: ~b~Tip:~b~ Call Joey from your ~b~Contacts~b~ ~BLIP_APP_CONTACTS~ to have your vehicle repaired or delivered.\r\nf22c2ff3: Joey\r\nf68a2f70: The largest of all hard-shelled turtles, the green sea turtle is a common sight around reefs and coastal waters.\r\nf8babbb0: Coastal, Urban, Wetland\r\nfab575e7: Coastal Auto Care\r\nfbd596f8: Coastal\r\n"}
